WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.500
I work with computers
and digital video a lot,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.080
which means that I think of color
in terms of light,

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.840
in terms of amounts
of red, green and blue.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.860
Someone who works with print
might think of color

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.300
in terms of combining inks,
cyan, magenta, yellow and black.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.740
A physicist might think in
terms of the frequencies

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.315
and wavelengths of the light,

00:00:17.315 --> 00:00:19.543
but there is a very
different way to look at it,

00:00:19.543 --> 00:00:23.730
through the chemistry and compounds
that make up colors.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.402
Inside those buildings,
the Harvard Art Museums,

00:00:26.402 --> 00:00:29.290
is the Forbes Pigment Collection.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.910
- The pigment collection was put together

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.890
by Edward Forbes, who was
the second director of the museum.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.800
He had been buying works of art

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.130
and in doing so he discovered
that the dealers in Italy

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.873
were seeing American collectors
as something of a mark.

00:00:43.873 --> 00:00:47.150
What he decided is that if you understand
what a work of art is made of,

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.300
what the original materials were
that an artist used,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:55.430
then you can tell original from restoration,
original from fake.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.040
And so what he did was
start buying pigments

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.414
to use as standards for the
analysis of works of art.

00:01:01.414 --> 00:01:05.687
Knowing that it was visible to the public
meant that I needed to make some sense

00:01:05.687 --> 00:01:08.160
of what we have as a collection.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.395
So what I did was take a color wheel,
open it out, have yellow in the centre

00:01:13.395 --> 00:01:17.259
and we go along one way to blue,
along the other way to red,

00:01:17.259 --> 00:01:20.160
and purple at each end.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.860
So we have unique colors
along the top,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.980
we have duplicates of those colors,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.940
which are chemical duplicates,
but not actual color duplicates

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.220
and then underneath on the
bottom shelf we have

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.790
the raw material that
makes up the colors above.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:40.180
In effect, what we have are the materials
that make up paint next to each other

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.400
and then if people look
at the galleries below,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.650
they can actually see what artists can do
with these raw materials.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:51.750
If you think about iron oxide for example,
hematite, but as it forms in the earth,

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.920
those slight additions that the earth adds
into the hematite deposits

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.160
allow it to look slightly different,

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.670
so we have 60 different
samples of hematite.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.830
Each of those is a slightly different
shade from the other.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.480
These pigments are not
used for restoration.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.670
We use them only as standards
for analyzing samples from works of art.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.810
By analyzing the materials,
we can understand

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:16.617
the thinking process and if the artist
is no longer alive,

00:02:16.617 --> 00:02:19.720
it's really the closest way to having an
interview with the artist.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.210
It's also great for teaching.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.350
We can show the students
how pigments change.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.717
They not just fade, but
some pigments darken.

00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:28.670
It's like Vaseline, but 80 years old.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.080
It doesn't last forever.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.327
Vermillion, red lead will turn black
on exposure to light.

00:02:34.327 --> 00:02:36.260
You can see how it started.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.280
And then other pigments like eosin,
which Van Gogh used a lot,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.080
will fade and give an
entirely different impression

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.500
of what the painting was to what
it looks like now.

00:02:48.503 --> 00:02:51.610
And so for security, we don't
have the public in here.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.210
Some of the pigments are toxic,
so we don't want people touching them,

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:55.043
playing with them.

00:02:55.043 --> 00:02:56.800
So pigments are made of mercury,

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.470
they're made of cadmium,
arsenic and so on.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:01.700
The oldest white pigment is lead white.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:07.210
It's made by taking lead metal,
putting it into a container with vinegar.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.970
That container is buried in cow dung,

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.890
so out of manure you get the most
pure beautiful white pigment.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.760
That's been around for
hundreds and hundreds of years.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.110
People used it as makeup.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.240
Lead white is toxic in the
way that lead is toxic.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:21.940
We have mummy brown.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:26.000
It has been used probably
since the 17th century

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.740
and it's made up of Egyptian mummies
that have been ground up into pigment.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.530
Indian yellow is an interesting pigment.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.120
It's made by feeding
cows mango leaves only

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.530
and collecting the urine
and drying the urine.

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.816
What you see on the screen
depends on the limitations

00:03:41.816 --> 00:03:44.980
of what the computer screen can depict.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.680
So videoing them,
putting them into the digital format,

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.280
doesn't replicate the color.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.960
There is innovation and people are
developing new ways to depict colors.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.500
So for example, Mas Subramanian developed
a blue pigment called YInMn Blue.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:01.850
He discovered it by accident.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:06.590
It's very stable and it's the first inorganic
blue pigment that's been invented

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.890
for a couple of hundred years.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.890
There's been a new black
that's come onto the market,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:14.190
which is Vantablack, which stands for
Vertically Arranged Nano-Tube Arrays.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:19.430
What you have is a forest of very tiny tubes
and light will go into that.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.870
It will bounce around inside the tubes
and then get issued as heat.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:29.200
It's a beautiful velvety looking surface
that doesn't bounce any light back.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.310
Chemists produce more and
more pigments every year

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:36.000
and I think that we're going to see
pigments depicting colors

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.540
that we never thought were possible.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.690
Every day somebody is coming in here,

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.440
taking a pigment out and using it
as a reference.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.540
This was beautifully
arranged when I installed it.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.460
It took me like four months
of lining everything up

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.970
and it's all a little
higgledy-piggledy now,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.492
so you can tell that
we use it all the time.

00:04:52.492 --> 00:04:55.010
It's not an historic artifact,

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.980
it's something that we rely
on to do our work properly.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:00.050
- My thanks to everyone at
the Harvard Art Museums.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.900
Pull down the description
for more about them

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.800
and more about the pigment collection.

